## Proof

**Key Lemma.** *For any digraph $G$ and any $i \geq 1$, every directed cycle of length $k$ in $G$ gives rise to a directed cycle of length $k$ in $L^{(i)}(G)$.*

*Proof.* Let $C = v_0 \to v_1 \to \cdots \to v_{k-1} \to v_0$ be a directed cycle in $G$ with all vertices distinct. The vertices of $L^{(i)}(G)$ are directed walks of length $i$ in $G$. For each $j \in \{0,\ldots,k-1\}$, define $W_j = (v_j, v_{j+1}, \ldots, v_{j+i})$ with indices modulo $k$. Each $W_j$ is a directed walk of length $i$ along $C$, hence a vertex of $L^{(i)}(G)$. Since the $v_j$ are all distinct, the walks $W_0, \ldots, W_{k-1}$ are all distinct. The arc $W_j \to W_{j+1 \bmod k}$ exists in $L^{(i)}(G)$ since $W_{j+1}$ extends $W_j$ by one step. Thus $W_0 \to W_1 \to \cdots \to W_{k-1} \to W_0$ is a directed cycle of length $k$ in $L^{(i)}(G)$. $\square$

**Theorem.** *A digraph $G$ satisfies the condition if and only if $G$ has no odd directed cycle.*

*Proof of $(\Leftarrow)$:* Suppose $G$ has no odd directed cycle. We show by induction on $i$ that $L^{(i)}(G)$ has no odd directed cycle for all $i \geq 1$.

**Base case** ($i=1$): Suppose for contradiction that $L(G)$ has an odd directed cycle of length $k$ (odd). This corresponds to a closed directed walk $v_0 \to v_1 \to \cdots \to v_k = v_0$ in $G$ with all arcs $(v_j, v_{j+1})$ distinct. This is a closed directed walk of odd length in $G$. Decomposing it into simple directed cycles, at least one constituent cycle must be odd (since the parities sum to odd)—contradicting that $G$ has no odd directed cycle.

**Inductive step:** If $L^{(i)}(G)$ has no odd directed cycle, then applying the base-case argument with $L^{(i)}(G)$ in place of $G$ shows $L^{(i+1)}(G)$ has no odd directed cycle.

Since $L^{(i)}(G)$ has no odd directed cycle for any $i$, its odd girth is $\infty \geq g$ for every positive integer $g$. Taking $i=1$ suffices for all $g$.

*Proof of $(\Rightarrow)$:* Suppose $G$ has an odd directed cycle of length $k$. By the Key Lemma, $L^{(i)}(G)$ has a directed cycle of length $k$ for every $i \geq 1$. Since $k$ is odd, the odd girth of $L^{(i)}(G)$ is at most $k$ for every $i$. Setting $g = k+1$, no $i$ yields odd girth $\geq g$, so the condition fails. $\square$

**Example.** The directed 2-cycle $G$ (vertices $\{a,b\}$, arcs $a \to b$, $b \to a$) has no odd directed cycle. By the theorem, $G$ satisfies the condition: $L^{(i)}(G)$ is a directed 2-cycle for every $i$, with odd girth $\infty$.

**Conclusion.** Yes, such a digraph exists. A digraph $G$ has the desired property if and only if $G$ contains no odd directed cycle; in that case, every iterated line digraph $L^{(i)}(G)$ is free of odd directed cycles, giving infinite odd girth for all $i$. Conversely, any odd directed cycle of length $k$ in $G$ persists in every $L^{(i)}(G)$, permanently bounding the odd girth by $k$.

